{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head d0b09ca2ffebe539874a7e2d5ab3005a33668d77, resting on all 87 artifacts with packages/room/README.md first. Independently read all 20 changed Room files versus A5, the lockfile delta, and credited the 43 unchanged Room artifacts and 23 inherited Git artifacts already reviewed in B/pushlog and jj. No source modifications remain in the detached candidate.\n\nP2 — A shared snapshot commit is incorrectly used as a unique reverse key to a canonical integration (src/admission.ts check(), src/obligations.ts underlyingIntegration()). recordSnapshots correctly permits multiple rows for the same deterministic snapshot commit, but both readers SELECT one row by commit_sha alone. An independent real Room/workerd test creates two active landings: both change src/app.ts to v2; the second also changes docs/a.md outside this checker's inputs. Their canonical integrations differ and their filtered snapshot commits are identical. The second landing's freshly run volatile check, naming its own recorded snapshot, exact paths/digest/config and landOp, is refused check-binding: 'The check does not bind an integration the room prepared for this generation.' Both mappings are present. This defeats legitimate reruns and becomes more common as scoped snapshots are reused. Resolve admission and evidence counting using the canonical integration/job or land operation together with checker/config/paths, rather than selecting an arbitrary reverse mapping. Preserve immutable snapshot sharing and reject a mismatched canonical/job association. Amendment 3's separate canonical integration and snapshot/job binding is the intended format. Test two concurrent integrations with identical snapshot objects, fresh volatile checks on each, and wrong-job/operation bindings.\n\nInherited P2 dependencies — this candidate's production Git package is byte-identical to pushlog revision 1 b013d648 (only the already approved jj tests/measurement/docs are added).